# 5. Types of Gradient Descent ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## A. Batch Gradient Descent

Uses the entire dataset for one update.

```text
Entire Dataset
      ↓
Calculate Gradient
      ↓
Update Model
```

### Advantages

✅ Stable gradient estimate  
✅ Deterministic for a fixed dataset/order  

### Disadvantages

❌ Slow on large datasets  
❌ Requires processing all data before each update  

---

# 6. Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD)

Updates parameters using one training example at a time.

```text
Data Point 1 → Update
Data Point 2 → Update
Data Point 3 → Update
```

### Advantages

✅ Fast updates  
✅ Works well with large/streaming data  
✅ Noise can help explore the optimization landscape  

### Disadvantages

❌ Noisy updates  
❌ Loss fluctuates  

---

# 7. Mini-Batch Gradient Descent ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Uses small batches:

```text
Batch 1 → Update
Batch 2 → Update
Batch 3 → Update
```

Example:

```text
Batch Size = 32
```

This is the most common approach in modern Deep Learning.

### Why?

It balances:

- Computational efficiency
- Gradient stability
- Hardware acceleration

---

# 📊 Comparison

| Type | Data per Update | Speed | Stability |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Batch GD | Entire dataset | Slower | High |
| SGD | 1 sample | Fast updates | Noisy |
| Mini-Batch | Small subset | Efficient | Good |

