# 21. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> Gradient Descent is an iterative optimization algorithm used to minimize a model's loss function. During each iteration, we compute the gradient of the loss with respect to the model parameters, which points in the direction of steepest increase. To reduce the loss, we update parameters in the opposite direction using a learning rate that controls the step size. Common variants include batch gradient descent, stochastic gradient descent, and mini-batch gradient descent. Modern optimizers such as Momentum, RMSProp, and Adam improve convergence by incorporating information from previous gradients or adapting update magnitudes.

---

# 22. Top 3 Interview Questions 🎯

## Q1. Why do we move opposite to the gradient?

> The gradient points in the direction of steepest increase of the loss function. Therefore, moving in the negative gradient direction locally decreases the loss most rapidly.

---

## Q2. What happens if the learning rate is too high?

> The optimization can overshoot the minimum, oscillate, or diverge instead of converging.

---

## Q3. Why is Mini-Batch Gradient Descent commonly used?

> It provides a balance between stable gradient estimates and computational efficiency. It also works efficiently with vectorized hardware such as GPUs.

